export const FR_C1_S01_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "fr-c1-le-voisin-du-dessus": [
    `Monday, February 3. He's started again. At ten past two in the morning, precisely, the scrape of a chair on the floorboards, then those footsteps, heavy, dragging, stubborn, going from the window to the door and from the door to the window, like a wild animal in a cage. I am now keeping this notebook on the advice of my son, who claims that you need evidence before you go and complain to the building management. Evidence I shall have. I taught history for thirty-seven years: I know what a dated source is worth.`,
    `Wednesday, February 5. Ran into the caretaker, Mme Ferreira, on the stairs. I asked her, casually, who lived above me. She looked at me with that expression people now wear when they speak to me, half fond, half worried, and she answered something I didn't catch because of the racket of the bins she was dragging. No matter. I slipped a courteous but firm note under the door upstairs, signed with my name and my former title.`,
    `Thursday, February 6. The nerve of this individual is beyond belief. This morning I found a note folded in four under my own door. It repeated the terms of my letter word for word, "Sir, I find myself obliged to inform you...", down to my signature, imitated with disturbing skill. A prankster, then, and a forger to boot. I laughed, and then I stopped laughing altogether. Last night the footsteps started again, slower than usual, and I thought I heard a voice, very low, calling someone.`,
    `Saturday, February 8. Visit from Julien. I showed him the note; he turned it over and over in his fingers without saying anything, then asked me whether I was sleeping well, whether I was taking my pills, whether I was eating. Past a certain age, our children start questioning us like country doctors. I reminded him that the issue was not my sleep but the sleep that was being stolen from me. In the end he offered to install a little camera in the flat, "to put our minds at rest". I agreed. A historian never turns down an archive.`,
    `Tuesday, February 11. Mme Ferreira brought my post up, which she never does, and she lingered on the landing, looking at the little service staircase that ends against a hatch sealed up for ages. I asked her whether she had finally identified my neighbor. She took my hand, which embarrassed me. "Monsieur Vasseur, you're on the sixth floor. Above you there's nothing but the roof."`,
    `I let it pass. Caretakers never go up into the attics; they have no idea what goes on there. How many garret rooms in these old silk-weavers' buildings of the Croix-Rousse have been let off the books to students, to artists, to people whose names appear on no letterbox? Up there is someone who knows my hours, my handwriting and my wife's first name. For that is the name the voice was calling the other night, I'm sure of it now: Hélène.`,
    `Friday, February 14. Julien came with his computer. He insisted that we watch the footage together, sitting down, and he kept his hand on my shoulder the whole time, the way you hold a child at the dentist's. It shows my living room in the blue light of two in the morning, and an old man in a checked dressing gown going from the window to the door, from the door to the window, pushing a chair in front of him. Now and then he stops, raises his head toward the ceiling and says something the microphone doesn't pick up. The dressing gown is mine. Hélène gave it to me for our fortieth wedding anniversary.`,
    `I said nothing to Julien. I pretended to understand; he looked so relieved. But I record here, for the record, a detail that escaped him: the man in the video doesn't look like me. He is more stooped, thinner, and there is something lost in his eyes that I have never had. He looks like my father, toward the end. Tonight I shall leave the chair by the window. We'll see who comes to fetch it.`,
  ],
  "fr-c1-le-diner-des-vertueux": [
    `At Clémence and Arnaud's, you don't entertain: you "share a moment". The nuance matters, and this Saturday's guests grasp it all the better because they use it themselves. The big flat in the eleventh arrondissement smells of sourdough bread, homemade of course, and of a beeswax candle bought at great expense from a beekeeper in the Cévennes who only sells to people he respects.`,
    `The first to arrive are Inès and Théo, by cargo bike, with their two children asleep in the wooden box and a bottle of natural wine whose hand-drawn label mentions neither the grape variety nor the vintage, which guarantees its authenticity. Next come Bastien and Margaux, who walked all the way from Belleville "on principle", and who make sure everyone knows it before they've even taken off their recycled linen scarves.`,
    `The aperitif begins like a game of chess. Arnaud advances a pawn: he now brews his own kombucha in the kitchen. Théo replies with a knight: they have given up the dishwasher, too thirsty for water. Margaux, without looking up from her glass, sacrifices a rook: she hasn't bought new clothes for three years, not even socks. There is an admiring silence, through which a hint of irritation shows. Clémence, who is wearing a designer dress, changes the subject with a diplomat's grace.`,
    `Over the main course, a curry of heirloom vegetables from a community-supported farm in Seine-et-Marne, the conversation drifts toward education. Everyone's children go to a school where they don't learn: they "awaken". Bastien tells how his six-year-old son refused a snack wrapped in plastic, and the whole table nods with restrained emotion, as if before a child prodigy. Inès confides that she feels guilty for still having a phone. They reassure her; they all feel guilty; that's how they recognize one another.`,
    `Then comes the sacred subject, the one saved for dessert the way others save the cognac: flying. Arnaud declares, with the sobriety of great confessions, that they no longer fly. Neither do Théo and Inès, of course. Bastien adds that Margaux and he did Portugal by night train last summer, thirty-two hours, and that it was "an incredible human experience". They talk about flight shame, rediscovered slowness, the joy of nearby destinations. That evening, each of them is a little purer than the next.`,
    `It is then that Capucine, nine, the hosts' daughter, bursts into the dining room in her pajamas, a brochure in her hand. She can't get to sleep, she has a question. She wants to know whether, in the Maldives, in February, there really will be the infinity pool you can see in the photo, and whether Jules, Inès's son, will be at the same hotel too, since Mummy said everyone was going together this year, "that way it's cheaper".`,
    `The silence that follows is of rare quality. Margaux studies her plate; Bastien discovers a sudden interest in the bookshelves. Arnaud steers his daughter back to her room with emphatic tenderness. When he returns, Clémence has recovered her diplomat's smile. She explains that they booked this trip ages ago, almost before they had become aware, and that in any case it would be offset: they'll plant trees, lots of trees, somewhere. Everyone agrees with relief. A second bottle of natural wine is opened, they drink a toast to future forests, and they part late, delighted with one another, promising to meet again by the pool.`,
  ],
  "fr-c1-l-horloger-de-la-rue-des-granges": [
    `For forty years Anselme Perrot had kept a little watchmaking workshop on the rue des Granges in Besançon, the town where for a long time watches had been made for the whole of France and where almost none were made any more. He was one of those craftsmen who speak little and look a great deal, eye screwed to the magnifying glass, and who end up confusing the beat of the balance wheels with that of their own hearts. He had never married. People in the neighborhood said he had married time, and he did not contradict them.`,
    `It was on a November 2 that she came in for the first time. She was about sixty, wore a grey coat, and placed a pocket watch on the counter the way one sets down a wounded bird. It had belonged to her husband, who had died in the spring. It had stopped, she said, and she couldn't bear to see it stopped. Anselme opened it, examined it, found nothing. The mainspring was fine, the wheels clean. He wound it, and it started again. He refused to be paid. She insisted, he refused again, and she stayed an hour telling him about her husband, a railwayman who had the habit of checking his watch even on Sundays.`,
    `The following year, on November 2, she came back. The watch had stopped again. This time Anselme found the cause: a grey hair, very fine, wound around the axle of the balance wheel. He removed it with his tweezers, without a word. He understood, and he understood too that he would say nothing. She talked to him about her husband again, then about other things, the cherry trees in her garden, a nephew who lived in Lausanne. He listened, the watch open between them like a pretext neither of them dared to close.`,
    `It went on for twelve years. Every November 2, Mme Garnier pushed open the workshop door and placed the watch on the counter, and each time Anselme found the same hair in it, whiter and whiter. He wound it, refused to be paid, and they talked. He learned that she loved detective novels and hated winter; she learned that he played the cello, badly, on Sunday evenings. Not once did he invite her for a coffee. Not once did she come on another day. It seemed to him that the slightest deviation would break a mechanism more delicate than any he had ever repaired.`,
    `In the thirteenth year, November 2 went by without her coming. Anselme stayed at his workbench until nine in the evening, the lamp on, his hands useless. The next day he told himself she was ill. The day after, he told himself she had grown tired of it, and that thought was more painful still. He had neither her address nor her number. He knew of her only her name, her hair and one hour of conversation a year, which, when he thought about it, was more than he knew about anyone.`,
    `It was a young woman who came in, in mid-December. She had Mme Garnier's eyes and held the watch in a handkerchief. Her grandmother had died in October, she said, of an illness she had hidden from everyone. She had left an envelope addressed to the watchmaker. Anselme opened it in front of her. There was just one line: "This time it stopped on its own. Keep it. I didn't know how else to come and see you."`,
    `He opened the watch. There was no hair. The spring had simply run down. He wound it and it started again, with the stubborn ticking of things that don't know they are being mourned. He did not put it in the window. He kept it on his workbench, next to the magnifying glass, and people in the neighborhood say that every November 2, the old watchmaker of the rue des Granges closes his shop at ten, opens the watch and stops it himself, for the length of a conversation.`,
  ],
  "fr-c1-la-plume-d-emprunt": [
    `In Abidjan they call me "the Poet of Cocody", which is flattering and inaccurate. I'm not a poet: I'm a public letter-writer, a profession people thought was dead and which is thriving. I write wedding speeches, eulogies, christening toasts, break-up letters and, on occasion, reconciliation letters for the same clients. I work in a maquis on the rue des Jardins, at a table at the back, between two plates of attiéké. My rates are reasonable and my tears guaranteed: if nobody cries, I give a refund. I have never given a refund.`,
    `Five years ago I was engaged to Aya. She left on a Tuesday, without anger, with a sentence I've never managed to refute: "You know how to say other people's words, Désiré. Your own, you keep to yourself." I found the phrase so apt that I almost offered to buy it from her.`,
    `In March, a young man sat down opposite me. Blue suit, Swiss watch, the quiet self-assurance of those who have never waited for a taxi in the rain. His name was Serge, he was an engineer, he was getting married in May and he had, he admitted, "no talent for feelings in public". He took out his phone to show me his fiancée. It was Aya. She was smiling on a beach at Grand-Bassam, and she looked happy in a way I had never seen in her.`,
    `I should have refused. A doctor doesn't operate on himself, and a letter-writer doesn't write another man's declaration of love to the woman he has lost. I said yes, and I doubled my rate, out of a kind of modesty. For three weeks I questioned Serge about their habits, their quarrels, their favorite dishes. He didn't know she was afraid of thunderstorms, that she sang out of tune on purpose to make people laugh, that she always read the end of a novel before the beginning. I knew. I gave him everything.`,
    `I wrote that speech at night, in my room in Yopougon, with a care I had never put at anyone's service. I put into it everything I hadn't told her while there was still time, and even a sentence I had whispered to her only once, one evening during a power cut: "You are the only place in the world where I don't feel a stranger." I reread it a hundred times. I left it in.`,
    `The wedding was in a big hotel on the Plateau. I had slipped in at the back of the room, among the distant cousins. Serge read my text diligently, stumbling over the commas, but the words did their work. The bride's mother cried from the second paragraph; the soldier uncle held out until the fourth. When Serge reached the power-cut sentence, Aya raised her head, and her eyes searched the room until they found mine. She didn't cry. She smiled, a smile I couldn't read.`,
    `Later, while the band was playing, she came over to me, her train gathered over her arm. "You've finally written something of your own," she said. I replied that I hadn't, that the text now belonged to her husband, that he had paid for it and no doubt deserved it more. She laid a hand on my arm, briefly, the way you check that a cup is no longer hot. Then she went back to dancing.`,
    `The next day I sent Serge my invoice. At the last moment, I crossed out the double rate and applied a fifty percent discount, with a note that nobody, I think, will ever understand: "Part of the text had already been used."`,
  ],
  "fr-c1-l-entretien": [
    `Chloé Marchetti, thirty-one, "Talent Acquisition Partner" at Kyvo, a Lyon start-up which, according to its website, is revolutionizing "the last-mile delivery experience", has a method. She got it from a two-day training course in Paris, and she applies it with the fervor of the converted: put the candidate at ease, spot their "soft skills", assess their "alignment with the company culture". In front of her this Thursday is one Michel Bertin, fifty-seven, an accountant, unemployed for eight months.`,
    `He is wearing a tie, which is already a signal. She was taught on her course that a tie often betrays a "vertical culture", which is to say, in plain language, a man who has had bosses and remembers it. At Kyvo nobody wears a tie, not even the founder, who comes to the office on a scooter in odd socks to underline his uniqueness. Chloé offers him a coffee, a tea, cucumber-infused water. He asks for a glass of tap water. She notes, discreetly: "not very open to experimentation".`,
    `"So, Michel, may I call you Michel? Here we're all on first-name terms, but anyway. If you had to describe yourself in three emojis?" He thinks for a long time, with a seriousness that makes her uncomfortable. "I'd say a calculator, a cup of coffee and an umbrella." She smiles, waits for the explanation. He doesn't give it. She presses him. "I count accurately, I start work early, and I've seen a lot of storms go by." She writes "atypical answer", then hesitates and adds a question mark.`,
    `Then comes her favorite question, the one that trips up even the most seasoned: "What is your greatest weakness?" A good candidate is expected to confess a flaw disguised as a quality, excessive perfectionism, too much commitment. Michel Bertin answers that he is slow. That he rereads everything twice. That this annoyed his former manager, until the day this slowness saved the company from a two-million-euro mistake. "After that, nobody criticized me for being slow. They fired me for other reasons."`,
    `It is at that moment that Chloé's computer lights up. A message from the founder, sent to the whole Human Resources team: from next quarter, the preselection of candidates will be "entrusted to a more efficient artificial intelligence solution". A workshop is planned for Friday to "support the transition". Chloé rereads the sentence twice, like Michel. She feels her cheeks burn. She closes the laptop a little too quickly.`,
    `Michel saw nothing, or pretends not to have. He simply asks whether she is all right. She says yes, then no, then, without really knowing why, she tells him. He listens, nodding, without interrupting, with a patience nobody at Kyvo has the time to have. Then he gives her three pieces of advice, very simple: sign nothing on Friday, ask in writing what is meant by "transition", and call, this very evening, a lawyer he knows on the place Bellecour, who got him a settlement twice as large as the one he had been offered.`,
    `The interview lasts an hour and a quarter, forty-five minutes longer than the recommended format. When Michel Bertin has left, his tie still impeccable, Chloé reopens the evaluation grid. In the "Recommendation" box she types "overqualified candidate, poor fit with our culture". She looks at the sentence for a long time. Then she deletes it and writes instead the only true thing she has written since joining the company: "Hire urgently. If possible, for me."`,
  ],
};

